= Sunk cost effect =

== Overview ==
The '''sunk cost effect''' is increased willingness to continue an activity because irrecoverable resources have already been invested.

== Normative benchmark ==
Under standard forward-looking decision analysis, costs that cannot be recovered should not affect which future option has the highest expected value.

== Mechanisms ==
Escalation can reflect loss framing, waste avoidance, self-justification, reputation, learning, or beliefs about future returns. Not every decision to continue after investment is a sunk-cost error.

== References ==
* Hal R. Arkes & Catherine Blumer, “The Psychology of Sunk Cost”, ''Organizational Behavior and Human Decision Processes'' 35 (1985): 124–140.

